plan FlowState
title   Flow State: entry plan and build order
status  proposed
goal
    Flow State combines statechart actors and keyed network data in one Runtime for applications
    written primarily by AI coding agents. A correct app preserves edits, keeps shared records
    coherent, publishes consistent views, isolates users and requests, and reports uncertain remote
    writes honestly. Its behaviour can be inspected structurally and replayed deterministically.

    Capability plans own their contracts, decisions and proofs:
    Machines — states, turns, guards and continuing work (@plan.Machines.O1).
    Runtime — apps, readiness, actors, messaging and context (@plan.Runtime.O1).
    Operations — reads, commits, streams, writes and lanes (@plan.Operations.O1).
    EntityDomains — coherent records, repair and predictions (@plan.EntityDomains.O1).
    Persistence — capture, restore and second-tab refusal (@plan.Persistence.O1).
    ServerRendering — request isolation and SSR hydration (@plan.ServerRendering.O1).
    LazyFeatures — deferred adoption and dormant durable data (@plan.LazyFeatures.O1).
    React — attachment, consistent views and component lifetime (@plan.React.O1).
    Testing — Stories, fixtures, checkpoints, models and properties (@plan.Testing.O1).
    Diagnostics — FlowError, codes and actionable failures (@plan.Diagnostics.O1).
    CommandLine — installed commands, checks and process behaviour (@plan.CommandLine.O1).
    Inspection — structural and live inspection, traces and safe evidence (@plan.Inspection.O1).
    Package — routes, dependency identity and installed consumers (@plan.Package.O1).

    references/dispatch-desk is the worked example built against these contracts, including a
    Next.js App Router host for hydration, reload restore, lazy features and second-tab refusal.
outcomes
    O1  Code disposition
        Rebuild packages/flow-state-rewrite in place. Port useful algorithms and behavioural test
        vectors, not superseded authoring surfaces or compatibility shims. packages/flow-state stays
        frozen; product cutover requires a separate gate.
    O2  Build stages
        Each prerequisite gate must pass before its dependent work starts. A proof that exercises a
        capability from a later stage (persistence, hydration, lazy adoption, exported evidence)
        runs at that later stage's gate, never earlier. Every stage gate includes the
        packed-consumer check in @plan.Package.O4 for the routes available at that stage.

        S1 Type spike — authoring types; needs none; gate @V1.
        S2 Foundation — diagnostics and structural compilation; needs S1; gate @V2.
        S3 Runtime — turns, actors, context and lifecycle; needs S2; gate @V3.
        S4 Operations — reads, commits, streams and lanes; needs S3; gate @V4.
        S5 Entity domains and property tests — needs S4 and S7; gate @V5.
        S6 Persistence — core actor and resource persistence needs S4; entity persistence needs S5;
        gate @V6.
        S7 Stories — Story, fixture, checkpoint and model infrastructure; needs S3; gate @V7.
        S8 React — attachment and consistent views; needs S3; gate @V8.
        S9 Inspection and CLI — needs S5 and S7; lazy-entry proofs run in S10; gate @V9.
        S10 SSR, lazy features and proving app — needs S6, S8 and S9; gate @V10.

decisions
    D1  Structure is data and compiles to one representation
        Targets, operations, outcome edges and dependencies are declared as data; callbacks compute
        values. Inspection and tools read the same compiled structure without running app behaviour.
    D2  One canonical form per concept
        A concept has one authoring form and one meaning across runtime, tools and tests.
    D3  Agents are the primary authors
        Likely authoring mistakes become type errors, check findings or actionable diagnostics;
        failures preserve valid application state.
    D4  Effect 4.0.5 is the authority; services are Layers
        Use its Schema, Layer, Duration, Schedule, Stream and KeyValueStore where applicable.
        Runtime services and Story fixtures are Effect Layers.
    D5  Authoring returns values and throws one FlowError
        Authoring calls return their values directly; invalid construction throws FlowError as
        specified by @plan.Diagnostics.O1.
    D6  Sensitive values never leave the Runtime raw
        A value is sensitive when its Schema is annotated sensitive: true or is Schema.Redacted, at
        any depth, entity fields included. Sensitive values are omitted from server-rendering
        payloads (@plan.ServerRendering.O3) and digested in exported evidence (@plan.Inspection.O5);
        in-process reads and persisted storage keep them faithfully (@plan.Persistence.D1).
constraints
    C1  Archived Plans are provenance only
        Nothing under workflow/plans/archive or workflow/tasks/archive establishes a requirement.
        Silence in a current plan does not import an archived rule; unresolved behaviour belongs in
        the owning capability plan.
    C2  Version 1 scope
        Include Next.js SSR hydration, lazy features and persistence with second-tab refusal
        (@plan.Persistence.O7). Exclude React Suspense, multi-tab sharing, sync engines and
        cross-origin sync.
    C3  Toolchain
        Use Node 22.18+, pinned nub@0.7.5, TypeScript 7.0.2, Vite Plus and Effect 4.0.5. No unpinned
        dependencies or erasure of public type information to satisfy proofs.
verification
    V1  S1: authoring type proofs pass (@plan.Machines.V1, @plan.Operations.V1,
        @plan.EntityDomains.V1, @plan.Testing.V1).
    V2  S2: diagnostics and structural proofs pass (@plan.Diagnostics.V1 to V4, @plan.Machines.V2).
    V3  S3: turn, actor, context and lifecycle proofs pass (@plan.Machines.V3 to V9,
        @plan.Runtime.V1 to V7).
    V4  S4: read, commit, stream and lane proofs pass (@plan.Operations.V2 to V10).
    V5  S5: entity domain and property-test proofs pass (@plan.EntityDomains.V2 to V5,
        @plan.Testing.V6).
    V6  S6: persistence proofs pass (@plan.Persistence.V1 to V9).
    V7  S7: Story, fixture, checkpoint and model proofs pass (@plan.Testing.V2 to V5, V8).
    V8  S8: React proofs pass (@plan.React.V1 to V5).
    V9  S9: inspection and CLI proofs pass (@plan.Diagnostics.V5, @plan.Inspection.V1 to V7,
        @plan.CommandLine.V1 to V7).
    V10  S10: SSR, lazy-feature, property and installed-consumer proofs pass
        (@plan.ServerRendering.V1 to V10, @plan.React.V6, @plan.LazyFeatures.V1 to V7,
        @plan.Testing.V7, @plan.Package.V1 to V4).
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        Algorithm port selection and module layout. Stage task breakdown and scheduling of the S7
        infrastructure gate. Performance measurements and budgets within the owning capability
        contracts.
